TP.register({id:"lp8-g3",lp:8,grade:"3",mode:"curriculum",checks:7,nextGrade:"4th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="The Seed Library";const FLU=["Every spring the town library sets out a wooden box by the front door. The box is not full of books. It is full of seeds.","Anyone may take a packet home. There are beans, carrots, zinnias, and sunflowers, and every packet is free. The only rule is written on the lid in black marker: if your plants do well, bring seeds back in the fall.","Ms. Okonkwo, the librarian, started the box nine years ago with seeds from her own garden. Now the box is so busy that she has to fill it twice a week.","This year the box has something new. A card taped to the side tells where each kind of seed grows best. Beans want a sunny row. Carrots want soft, loose dirt. Zinnias do not mind the heat, and sunflowers need room to lean.","She keeps a notebook next to the box. In it she writes the name of every plant that comes back, and the year it came. Some names show up again and again. A few show up once and never again, and she circles those so she will remember to ask about them.","Last April a boy carried home one bean seed in his pocket. He counted the days. In October he came back with a jar of beans and poured every one of them into the box.","Ms. Okonkwo says the box is really a kind of library after all. A book goes out and comes back. A seed goes out and comes back as a hundred seeds."].join("\n\n");const STORY_TITLE="The Row That Would Not Come Up";const STORY=["Dev picked the last row in the school garden because nobody else wanted it. It was the row closest to the fence. The fence threw a shadow over it until almost noon.","He planted twenty sunflower seeds in that row on the first Monday in April. Ms. Reyes gave him a watering can and a wooden stake with his name on it.","By the next Monday, every other row in the garden had green in it. Dev's row was bare dirt.","He watered it anyway, every morning before the bell. He did not tell anyone that he had started counting the days.","On the fourteenth morning he found nine sprouts standing in a crooked line, each one about as tall as his thumb.","At lunch he brought Ms. Reyes out to look. “Nine out of twenty,” he said, and he was not sure if that was good. Ms. Reyes counted them again. “In the shadiest row in the garden,” she said, “nine is nine.”"].join("\n\n");const WAKE_TITLE="The Garden Wakes Up";const WAKE=["Rosa's grandfather called the March garden a sleeping bear. Nothing moved in it and nothing was green.","Every Saturday they walked the rows anyway. Her grandfather pushed one finger into the soil and said the same two words. “Not yet.”","Then it rained for three days. The rain drummed on the porch roof so hard that Rosa could not hear the radio.","On Sunday morning she went out in her boots. The bean row had come up overnight, rows of small green hands pushing out of the mud.","“Well,” her grandfather said. “The bear is awake.”","Rosa's heart did a little hop. She had waited all month, and the waiting had crawled, but now she could not stand still."].join("\n\n");const BEE_TITLE="Why Flowers Need Bees";const BEE=["A flower is not only pretty. A flower is how a plant makes seeds. To start a seed, a flower needs pollen, a fine yellow dust, to travel from one flower to another flower of the same kind.","Pollen cannot walk. Most of the time an animal carries it. A bee lands on a flower to drink the sweet nectar inside. While it drinks, pollen sticks to the tiny hairs on its legs and body. When the bee flies to the next flower, some of that pollen rubs off there.","Bees are not the only helpers. Butterflies, moths, beetles, flies, and even some birds and bats move pollen too. Any animal that moves pollen is called a pollinator.","Pollinators matter to people. About one of every three bites of food we eat comes from a plant that an animal pollinated. Apples, blueberries, almonds, and squash all need that help."].join("\n\n");const beeHtml=small=>F.passage(BEE,BEE_TITLE,!!small);const WIND_TITLE="Seeds on the Wind";const WIND=["Every seed has the same job. It has to get away from the plant it came from. A seed that drops straight down sits in its parent's shade and has to share its parent's water.","Some seeds ride the wind. A maple seed grows a thin paper wing. The wing makes the seed spin as it falls, the spinning slows the fall, and a gust has time to carry it off.","A dandelion seed hangs under a ring of white hairs, like a tiny parachute. It weighs so little that a steady breeze can take it clear across a field.","Wind costs the plant nothing. But the wind does not aim. Most seeds that ride it land on a road or a roof or a pond, where nothing can grow."].join("\n\n");const RIDE_TITLE="Seeds That Hitch a Ride";const RIDE=["Every seed has the same job. It has to get away from the plant it came from. A seed that drops straight down sits in its parent's shade and has to share its parent's water.","Some seeds travel on animals instead. A burdock seed head is covered with hundreds of tiny hooks. The hooks catch on fur, or on a sock, and the seed rides until it is pulled off.","Other seeds travel inside an animal. A bird swallows a berry. The soft part is food, and the hard seeds pass through the bird and land far from the bush.","A squirrel buries acorns to keep for winter and then forgets where some of them are. A forgotten acorn is already planted.","Riding an animal is not free. First the plant has to grow a sweet berry or a hooked burr. But an animal walks or flies, so the seed usually ends up somewhere a plant can grow."].join("\n\n");const twoTexts=()=>`<div class="texts" style="display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap"><article style="flex:1 1 320px">${F.passage(WIND,"Text 1: "+WIND_TITLE,true)}</article><article style="flex:1 1 320px">${F.passage(RIDE,"Text 2: "+RIDE_TITLE,true)}</article></div>`;const WORDS=["sunflower","rainfall","replant","unripe","mislabel","dampness","careful","rootless","direction","collection","measure","beautiful"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.4rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const stackFig=inner=>`<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;align-items:center;margin-top:10px">${inner}</div>`;function noteBoxes(a,b){const bx=t=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;min-width:250px;min-height:100px;padding:8px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0;font-size:1.35rem">${t}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure">${bx(a)}${bx(b)}</div>`}function xPlot(o){const min=o.min,max=o.max,step=o.step,W=o.width||560,x0=46,x1=W-46;const X=v=>x0+(v-min)/(max-min)*(x1-x0);const n=Math.round((max-min)/step),counts=o.counts||{},labels=o.labels||{};const maxc=Math.max(1,...Object.values(counts)),base=70+maxc*24;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${base+34}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt||"Line plot"}">`;s+=`<line x1="${x0-16}" y1="${base-40}" x2="${x1+16}" y2="${base-40}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/>`;for(let i=0;i<=n;i++){const v=+(min+i*step).toFixed(4),x=X(v);s+=`<line x1="${x}" y1="${base-48}" x2="${x}" y2="${base-32}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<text x="${x}" y="${base-10}" font-size="15" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${labels[v]!==void 0?labels[v]:v}</text>`;const c=counts[v]||0;for(let k=0;k<c;k++){const cy=base-60-k*24,r=7;s+=`<line x1="${x-r}" y1="${cy-r}" x2="${x+r}" y2="${cy+r}" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/><line x1="${x+r}" y1="${cy-r}" x2="${x-r}" y2="${cy+r}" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>`}}if(o.title)s+=`<text x="${W/2}" y="${base+26}" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}const BARREL_TITLE="What a Rain Barrel Does";const BARREL=["A rain barrel is a large container that stands under the downspout at the corner of a roof. When it rains, the water running off the roof pours into the barrel instead of running away down the driveway.","A roof catches far more water than most people expect. Half an inch of rain falling on a garage roof can fill a whole barrel to the top.","A screen across the opening keeps leaves and insects out of the water. A tap near the bottom lets a gardener fill a watering can straight from the barrel.","The water costs nothing, and it is sitting there waiting on the dry weeks, which is exactly when a garden needs it most."].join("\n\n");function areaFig(){const cell=36,rows=4,cols=10,w=cols*cell+16,h=rows*cell+44;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="${Math.min(w,580)}" role="img" aria-label="A garden bed 4 squares tall and 10 squares across. A heavy line after the seventh column splits it into a larger left part labelled Part A and a smaller shaded right part labelled Part B. Every square is one square foot.">`;for(let r=0;r<rows;r++)for(let c=0;c<cols;c++)s+=`<rect x="${8+c*cell}" y="${8+r*cell}" width="${cell}" height="${cell}" fill="${c<7?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--gold)"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>`;s+=`<path d="M${8+7*cell} 8 v${rows*cell}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="4.5"/>`;s+=`<text x="${8+3.5*cell}" y="${rows*cell+32}" font-size="19" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">Part A</text>`;s+=`<text x="${8+8.5*cell}" y="${rows*cell+32}" font-size="19" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">Part B</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Seed Packet Words",short:"Packet Words",parts:[{std:["RF.3.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:75,dir:"Read each word out loud. Look for a part you already know at the start or the end of the word.",desc:"reading longer words with prefixes, suffixes, and more than one syllable",key:["Read each word in order: sunflower, rainfall, replant, unripe, mislabel, dampness, careful, rootless, direction, collection, measure, beautiful.","Watch for: compound words (sunflower, rainfall), prefixes re-, un-, mis- (replant, unripe, mislabel), suffixes -ness, -ful, -less (dampness, careful, rootless), the -tion ending (direction, collection), and two irregular spellings where ea does not say long e (measure) and eau spells the long u sound (beautiful).","Optional word-part check: ask what re- does in replant (plant it again) and what -less does in rootless (without roots)."]}]},{id:2,title:"The Planting Line",short:"Seed Rows",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.2"],dur:140,dir:"Look at the rows. Answer out loud. Tell how you know.",desc:"naming a fraction as a number on a number line and placing fractions on a number line",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Each line below is one garden row. The 0 is the gate end. The 1 is the fence end. The dot is where one seed went in.</p>`,fig:()=>stackFig(group(F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:1/3,hideNumbers:true,ticks:[[0,"0"],[1,"1"]],marks:[2/3],width:560}),"Row A, cut into 3 equal parts")+group(F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:1/8,hideNumbers:true,ticks:[[0,"0"],[1,"1"]],marks:[1/8],width:560}),"Row B, cut into 8 equal parts")+group(F.numberLine({min:0,max:2,step:.25,labelEvery:1,marks:[1.25],width:560}),"Row C, two rows long, cut into fourths")),qs:["Row A is cut into 3 equal parts. What fraction of the way down the row is the seed?","Row B is cut into 8 equal parts. Name the fraction where the seed is. What is one part of this row called?","A row runs from 0 to 1. It is cut into 6 equal parts. How many jumps of 1/6 does it take? You get from 0 to 4/6.","Row C runs from 0 to 2 and is cut into fourths. Name the fraction where the seed is.","Tell your teacher how to draw a number line from 0 to 1 and mark 3/4 on it."],key:["2/3. The row from 0 to 1 is cut into 3 equal parts and the dot sits at the end of the second part. Partial: 2/3 right with no account of the equal parts is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)","1/8. One part of this row is 1/8, because the whole from 0 to 1 is cut into 8 equal parts. Partial: the fraction right with no name for one part is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)","4 jumps. Each jump is 1/6 of the row, so 4 jumps land on 4/6. Partial: 4 given with no account of the size of a jump is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)","5/4. Accept 1 1/4. Counting fourths from 0 gives 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 4/4, 5/4, and the dot is on the fifth one. Partial: 5/8 treats the whole 0 to 2 line as one whole and does not count. (3.NF.A.2)","Cut the space from 0 to 1 into 4 equal parts, then count 3 parts from 0 and mark that point. The mark goes at the end of the third part, not at the third tick counting the 0. Partial: counting ticks from the 0 instead of counting parts is the slip to watch and is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Read the Seed Library",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.3.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:175,dir:"Read the story out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level story read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Spring grade 3 benchmark: about 112 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, spring 50th percentile). 25th percentile is about 91 and 90th percentile is about 166. This is the last check of the year, so the spring column is the right one. The passage is long enough that a reader at the 90th percentile still has text left when the minute ends, so the score is not capped by the page.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the four-item list of seeds, for the colon before the rule on the lid, and for a slower, settled voice in the last two sentences."]}]},{id:4,title:"Why Flowers Need Bees",short:"Pollen",parts:[{std:["RI.3.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the text. Then answer out loud. Look back at the text to prove each answer.",desc:"questions about an informational text answered with the exact words in the text",html:()=>beeHtml(true),fig2:()=>F.row([F.icon("bee",110),"A bee carries pollen from flower to flower"],[F.icon("purpleFlower",110),"A flower is how a plant makes seeds"]),qs:["What is pollen? Read the words from the text.","Where does pollen stick on a bee? Read the sentence that tells you.","What is any animal that moves pollen called? Find the word in the text.","About how much of the food we eat comes from a plant an animal pollinated? Read the words that tell you.","Your turn to be the teacher. Ask one question that this text answers. Start with How, What, or Why."],key:["A fine yellow dust. Reads “pollen, a fine yellow dust.” (RI.3.1)","On the tiny hairs on its legs and body. Reads “pollen sticks to the tiny hairs on its legs and body.” (RI.3.1)","A pollinator. Reads “Any animal that moves pollen is called a pollinator.” (RI.3.1)","About one of every three bites. Reads “About one of every three bites of food we eat comes from a plant that an animal pollinated.” (RI.3.1)","Asks a question the text can answer, such as: What does a bee drink from a flower? Which animals besides bees move pollen? Why does a flower need pollen? (RI.3.1, asks questions)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Which Can Holds More?",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.3"],dur:150,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell how you know. You can draw on your paper to show your thinking.",desc:"explaining equivalent fractions and comparing fractions by reasoning about their size",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The garden crew writes down how full each watering can is as a fraction. Bar A and Bar B below are the same size. So they stand for two cans that are the same size.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:28px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.fracBar(3,1,{width:300}),"Bar A")}${group(F.fracBar(6,2,{width:300}),"Bar B")}</div>`,qs:["Which is more water in the same can, 3/4 or 3/8? How do you know?","Which is more, 5/6 of a can or 2/6 of the same can? How do you know?","Look at Bar A and Bar B. Are the shaded parts equal? Say it as two fractions that are equal.","Name a fraction that is equal to 2/4.","Write 1 whole as a fraction with 3 as the bottom number. Then write the whole number 6 as a fraction.","Two beds are not the same size. Ines says 1/4 of the small bed holds more seeds than 1/4 of the big bed. Can you settle that by comparing the two fractions? Tell why or why not."],key:["3/4. The top numbers are the same, so compare the piece sizes: fourths are bigger pieces than eighths, so 3 fourths is more than 3 eighths. Partial: naming 3/4 with no reason about the size of the pieces is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)","5/6. The pieces are the same size, so the one with more pieces is more. Partial: naming 5/6 with no reason is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)","Yes, they are equal. 1/3 = 2/6. The bars are the same size and the shaded parts cover the same amount. Partial: saying yes with no pair of equal fractions written is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)","1/2. Or: 3/6 or 4/8 counts as well. (3.NF.A.3)","3/3 and 6/1. Partial: one of the two given is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)","No. Fractions can only be compared when they name parts of the same whole. The beds are different sizes, so 1/4 of one is not the same amount as 1/4 of the other, and the two fractions alone cannot settle it. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the same whole is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)"]}]},{id:6,title:"The Row That Would Not Come Up",short:"Last Row",parts:[{std:["RL.3.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Look back at the story to prove each answer.",desc:"asking and answering story questions using the exact words in the text",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["Why did Dev pick the last row? Read the words from the story.","How many seeds did he plant, and when? Read the sentence that tells you.","What did Dev do every morning before the bell? Find the words in the story.","What did he find on the fourteenth morning? Read the words that tell you.","Your turn to be the teacher. Ask one question that this story answers. Start with Who, What, or Why."],key:["Reads “because nobody else wanted it.” Also correct: “the fence threw a shadow over it until almost noon.” (RL.3.1)","Reads “He planted twenty sunflower seeds in that row on the first Monday in April.” (RL.3.1)","Watered it. Reads “He watered it anyway, every morning before the bell.” (RL.3.1)","Reads “nine sprouts standing in a crooked line, each one about as tall as his thumb.” (RL.3.1)","Asks a question the story can answer, such as: Who gave Dev the watering can? What was on the wooden stake? Why was Dev's row still bare dirt? (RL.3.1, asks questions)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Measure the Sprouts",short:"Measure",parts:[{std:["3.MD.B.4"],dur:235,dir:"Use the rulers for numbers 1 to 3. For numbers 4 to 6, work on your paper and hold it up. For number 7, get a ruler and measure.",mats:["a ruler"],desc:"measuring to the nearest quarter inch, generating measurements of real objects with a ruler, and making a line plot of the measurements",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The class cut a paper strip exactly as tall as each sprout and laid the strip on a ruler. Every mark on these rulers is a quarter of an inch.</p>`,fig:()=>stackFig(group(F.ruler({length:5,unit:"in",item:3.75,itemLabel:"Sprout A",parts:4}),"Sprout A")+group(F.ruler({length:5,unit:"in",item:2.5,itemLabel:"Sprout B",parts:4}),"Sprout B")+group(F.ruler({length:5,unit:"in",item:1.25,itemLabel:"Sprout C",parts:4}),"Sprout C")),qs:["How tall is Sprout A, to the nearest quarter inch?","How tall is Sprout B?","How tall is Sprout C?","Here are ten more heights the class measured, in inches: 2, 2 1/4, 2 1/4, 2 1/2, 2 3/4, 2 3/4, 2 3/4, 3, 3, and 3 1/4. Make a line plot of them on your paper. Mark your scale in quarter inches from 2 to 3 1/4.","Use your line plot. Which height came up most often. How many sprouts were that tall? How many sprouts were taller than 2 3/4 inches?","How much taller was the tallest of those ten sprouts than the shortest?","Now you are the measurer. Find three small things at your table. Something like a pencil. Or an eraser. Or a paper clip. Measure each one with your ruler. Say what each thing is. Say how long it is, to the nearest quarter inch."],key:["3 3/4 inches. The strip ends three ticks past the 3. Partial: 4 inches rounds to a whole and is approaching. (3.MD.B.4)","2 1/2 inches. The strip ends halfway between 2 and 3, which is two quarter ticks past the 2. Partial: 2 inches or 3 inches rounds to a whole and is approaching. (3.MD.B.4)","1 1/4 inches. The strip ends one tick past the 1. Partial: 1 inch rounds to a whole and is approaching. (3.MD.B.4)","The scale needs every quarter inch from 2 to 3 1/4 marked and labeled, with no gaps: 2, 2 1/4, 2 1/2, 2 3/4, 3, 3 1/4. The marks go 1 above 2, 2 above 2 1/4, 1 above 2 1/2, 3 above 2 3/4, 2 above 3, and 1 above 3 1/4. Ten marks in all. A plot that leaves out 2 1/2 or 3 1/4, or that spaces the labels unevenly, is not yet correct. (3.MD.B.4, showing measurement data on a line plot)","2 3/4 inches came up most often, with 3 sprouts. 3 sprouts were taller than 2 3/4 inches: the two at 3 inches and the one at 3 1/4 inches. Partial: the most common height right with no count of taller sprouts is approaching. (3.MD.B.4, reading a line plot)","1 1/4 inches. The tallest was 3 1/4 and the shortest was 2, and 3 1/4 take away 2 is 1 1/4. Partial: naming the tallest and the shortest with no difference worked out is approaching. (3.MD.B.4)","Three objects of the student's own choosing, each measured and named with a length. Watch the 0 end of the ruler line up with the end of the object, and listen for a length to the nearest quarter inch (a whole, 1/4, 1/2, or 3/4) with the unit inches said. Write the three objects and lengths on the record sheet, and have the student hold one object on the ruler up to the camera so you can check that reading yourself. Or: any three objects count, since these are the student's own data and there is no fixed answer. Partial: three lengths given but all rounded to whole inches, or the ruler started at the 1 instead of the 0, is approaching. (3.MD.B.4, generating measurement data with a ruler)"]}]},{id:8,title:"A Sleeping Bear",short:"Bear",parts:[{std:["RL.3.4"],dur:125,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Use the story to explain each answer.",desc:"word and phrase meanings in a story, telling literal from nonliteral language",html:()=>F.passage(WAKE,WAKE_TITLE,true),qs:["Rosa's grandfather calls the March garden a sleeping bear. Is there really a bear in the garden? What does he mean?","The story says the bean row came up as rows of small green hands. Are there really hands in the mud? What does this tell you?","The rain drummed on the porch roof. What does drummed tell you about the rain that fell would not tell you?","The story says the waiting had crawled. Did the waiting really crawl? What does that tell you about Rosa?","One of these means exactly what the words say and one does not. Which is which? A: “On Sunday morning she went out in her boots.” B: “Rosa's heart did a little hop.”"],key:["Nonliteral. The garden is still and not growing, the way a bear is still all winter, and like a bear it will wake up. His later line, “The bear is awake,” is the same comparison. (RL.3.4, nonliteral language)","Nonliteral. The new bean leaves are small, green, and opening up out of the soil, so they look like little hands. (RL.3.4, nonliteral language)","Drummed says the rain hit hard and fast in a steady beat, loud enough to cover the radio. Fell would only say it came down. (RL.3.4, meaning of a word as used in the text)","Nonliteral. It means the month went by very slowly for her, because she was waiting for the garden to grow. (RL.3.4, nonliteral language)","A is literal, because she really did put boots on and walk outside. B is nonliteral, because a heart cannot hop. It means she felt a quick jump of happiness. (RL.3.4, literal and nonliteral)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Two Ways to Travel",short:"Two Texts",parts:[{std:["RI.3.9"],dur:140,dir:"Read both texts. They are about the same topic. Then answer out loud. Say which text each answer comes from.",desc:"comparing and contrasting the key points and details in two texts on the same topic",html:twoTexts,qs:["Both texts start with the same big idea. What is it?","Both texts give the same reason a seed needs to get away from its parent plant. What is the reason?","Name one key detail that is in Text 1 only.","Name one key detail that is in Text 2 only.","Text 1 says its way of traveling costs the plant nothing. Text 2 says its way does cost the plant something. What does Text 2 say the plant has to do first?","Your sock comes in from a walk covered in prickly seeds. Which text explains that, and which detail in it tells you?"],key:["Every seed has the same job: it has to get away from the plant it came from. Both texts open with that sentence. (RI.3.9, the point both texts share)","A seed that drops straight down sits in its parent's shade and has to share its parent's water. Both texts give that reason. (RI.3.9, a shared key detail)","Any detail from Text 1 only: a maple seed grows a thin paper wing that makes it spin; the spinning slows the fall so a gust can carry it; a dandelion seed hangs under a ring of white hairs like a tiny parachute; a breeze can carry it across a field; the wind does not aim, so most of those seeds land on a road, a roof, or a pond. (RI.3.9, a detail in one text only)","Any detail from Text 2 only: a burdock seed head has hundreds of tiny hooks that catch on fur or a sock; a bird swallows a berry and the hard seeds pass through and land far from the bush; a squirrel buries acorns and forgets some, and a forgotten acorn is already planted; an animal walks or flies, so the seed usually ends up somewhere a plant can grow. (RI.3.9, a detail in one text only)","The plant has to grow a sweet berry or a hooked burr first. (RI.3.9, contrasting the two texts)","Text 2. The detail is the burdock seed head covered with hundreds of tiny hooks that catch on fur or on a sock. (RI.3.9, choosing between two texts on one topic)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Fix the Garden Notice",short:"Fix It",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.3.1"],dur:80,dir:"Each line from the garden notice has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"fixing spoken grammar mistakes (plurals, verb tense, agreement, comparing words, pronouns)",qs:["The leafs on the bean plants are getting bigger.","Last spring we growed carrots in the back bed.","Six sunflowers was planted by the fence.","This is the most tallest sprout in the whole row.","Nadia filled her watering can, but she forgot their gloves."],key:["The leaves on the bean plants are getting bigger. (L.3.1, irregular plural noun)","Last spring we grew carrots in the back bed. (L.3.1, irregular past tense verb)","Six sunflowers were planted by the fence. (L.3.1, subject-verb agreement)","This is the tallest sprout in the whole row. (L.3.1, superlative adjective)","Nadia filled her watering can, but she forgot her gloves. (L.3.1, pronoun that matches its noun)"]},{title:"Build a Line",std:["L.3.1"],dur:60,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"spoken grammar items (joining sentences, future tense, adverbs, abstract nouns)",qs:["Join these two sentences into one. Use because: Dev watered the row every day. The soil dried out fast.","Change this sentence so it tells about tomorrow: The class plants the beans and waters the bed.","Which word fits? The rain fell ____ all night. Is it steady or steadily?","Which word names something you cannot see or touch: soil, trowel, or patience?"],key:["Dev watered the row every day because the soil dried out fast. (L.3.1, subordinating conjunction that joins a cause to a result)","The class will plant the beans and will water the bed. Both verbs need to change, so accept “will plant the beans and water the bed” too. (L.3.1, simple future tense)","steadily (L.3.1, adverb that tells how)","patience (L.3.1, abstract noun)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Tell the Garden Report",short:"Report",parts:[{std:["SL.3.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:135,dir:"Look at the chart for 1 minute. Then give your report out loud. Talk for about 1 minute. Speak clearly and not too fast.",desc:"a short spoken report on a topic with facts and descriptive details, at an understandable pace",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A family is visiting the school on Friday and wants to hear about the garden. Give them a report about one minute long. Use at least three facts from the chart, and add details that help them picture the garden.</p>${F.table(["Our school garden this spring","Number"],[["Raised beds","6"],["Rows in each bed","4"],["Seed packets planted","38"],["Kinds of vegetable growing","9"],["Gallons the rain barrel holds","55"],["Classes that take turns watering","3"]])}<div class="row center" style="margin-top:14px;gap:14px">${["What the garden is","Three facts from the chart","A detail they can picture","How it ends"].map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`,key:["Reports on the topic the task names, the school garden, and stays on it for about a minute (SL.3.4)","Uses at least three facts from the chart, stated correctly: 6 raised beds, 4 rows in each bed, 38 seed packets, 9 kinds of vegetable, a 55 gallon rain barrel, 3 classes watering. A changed number does not count. (SL.3.4, appropriate facts)","Adds at least one relevant descriptive detail that is not in the chart, such as how the beds smell after rain, how tall the sunflowers are now, or where the barrel sits (SL.3.4, relevant descriptive details)","The report has an order a listener can follow: it opens by saying what the garden is and closes instead of just stopping (SL.3.4)","Speaks clearly and at an understandable pace, loud enough to hear, without rushing (SL.3.4)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Pollen Notes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.3.8"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:100,dir:"Read the text. Write your notes on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"taking brief notes from a text and sorting them into given categories",html:()=>`${beeHtml(true)}<p class="big">Make two boxes on your paper. Label them like the boxes below. Write two short notes from the text in each box.</p>${noteBoxes("What pollen does","Who moves pollen")}<p class="small" style="text-align:center;font-size:1.2rem">A note is just a few words. You do not need a whole sentence.</p>`,key:["Pollen box: two facts, such as pollen is a fine yellow dust, it has to travel flower to flower, it starts a seed, pollen cannot walk. (W.3.8, sorts evidence into the given category)","Mover box: two facts, such as bees carry it on leg and body hairs, butterflies, moths, beetles, flies, some birds and bats, an animal that moves pollen is a pollinator. (W.3.8, sorts evidence into the given category)","Notes are brief: a few words or a short phrase, not a whole sentence copied out of the text. (W.3.8, brief notes)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Spring Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Rain barrel or more beds?"},std:["W.3.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">The school has saved enough money for one new thing in the garden this spring. It can buy a second rain barrel, or it can build two more raised beds. A second barrel would catch roof water so the garden could be watered on dry weeks. Two more beds would give two more classes a place to plant.</p><p class="big">Which should the school pick? Write your opinion and give reasons. Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Say your opinion in the first sentence","Give two reasons for it","Use a linking word: because, since, also, for example","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear opinion about the barrel or the beds (W.3.1)","Gives reasons that support the opinion, such as water on dry weeks or room for two more classes (W.3.1)","Uses linking words such as because, since, also, for example, therefore (W.3.1)","Ends with a concluding sentence (W.3.1)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"🌱",hint:"The morning the first sprout showed"},std:["W.3.3"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You planted something weeks ago and nothing came up. Then one morning you go out to look and there it is. Write the story of that morning. Write 5 or 6 sentences.</p>${pills("First","Next","After that","Finally")}${checklist(["Tell who is there and where it happens","Tell what happens in order","Add a detail that helps the reader picture it, and tell what someone says or feels","Give your story an ending"])}`,key:["Sets up the situation and who is in the story (W.3.3)","Events happen in a clear order, with time words such as first, next, after that, finally (W.3.3)","Uses descriptive details and tells thoughts or feelings, or uses dialogue (W.3.3)","Has an ending that wraps up the story (W.3.3)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Trays of Seedlings",short:"Groups",parts:[{std:["3.OA.A.1","3.OA.A.2"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the trays. Write each equation on your paper and say what the numbers mean.",desc:"interpreting a product as groups of equal size, and a quotient as a share size and as a number of shares",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The nursery sets out its seedlings in trays. Every tray holds the same number.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row(F.array(6,4,"sprout")),qs:["Write a multiplication equation for this picture. Say what each number in it stands for.","Now 24 seedlings are shared out equally among 6 trays. Write the division equation. What does the answer stand for?","Say the same division a different way. 24 seedlings are put into trays that hold 6 each. Now what does the answer stand for?"],key:["6 x 4 = 24 (3.OA.A.1). The 6 is the number of trays, the 4 is how many seedlings are in each tray, and 24 is the total. Reading the picture the other way, 4 x 6 = 24 with 4 columns of 6, is correct too as long as the student says which number is the groups and which is the size of a group. Partial: the equation right with no account of what each number stands for is approaching.","24 / 6 = 4 (3.OA.A.2). Here the 4 is how many seedlings go in each tray, because the 24 are split into 6 equal shares. Partial: the equation right with no account of what the answer stands for is approaching.","24 / 6 = 4 again, but now the 4 is the number of trays, because the 24 are put into groups of 6 (3.OA.A.2). The same equation answers two different questions, and that is the thing to listen for. Partial: repeating the first reading instead of the number of trays does not count here."]}]},{id:15,title:"Nursery Two-Steppers",short:"Two Steps",parts:[{std:["3.OA.D.8"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Solve each one on your paper. Write an equation with a letter for the unknown. Say your answer.",desc:"two-step word problems with an equation using a letter for the unknown, and a rounding check",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The plant nursery down the road keeps its own count.</p>`,qs:["The nursery had 112 bean plants on the bench. It sold 38 on Friday and 27 on Saturday. How many are left?","The nursery fills 8 flats with 15 seedlings in each flat. Then puts 36 loose seedlings on the cart as well. How many seedlings in all?","Use rounding to check the second answer. Is it reasonable?"],key:["47 bean plants. 38 + 27 = 65, then 112 - 65 = 47. An equation such as 112 - (38 + 27) = p, p = 47, or two equations with a letter in each, both count. The letter can be anything. Partial: 74 takes off only one of the two days and is approaching.","156 seedlings. 8 x 15 = 120, then 120 + 36 = 156. An equation such as s = 8 x 15 + 36. Partial: 120 stops at the flats and leaves the loose seedlings out, so it is approaching.","Yes. 8 x 15 is 120 and 36 rounds to 40, so the answer should be about 160. 156 is close to 160, so it is reasonable. Any clear estimate near 150 to 160 counts. Listen for the student comparing the estimate to the answer, not just producing an estimate. Partial: an estimate with no comparison to the answer is approaching."]}]},{id:16,title:"Round the Count",short:"Rounding",parts:[{std:["3.NBT.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the number line. Answer out loud.",desc:"rounding whole numbers to the nearest 10 and the nearest 100",fig:()=>F.row(F.numberLine({min:640,max:660,step:1,labelEvery:5,width:600,marks:[647]})),qs:["The dot is on 647. Round it to the nearest 10. Then round 647 to the nearest 100.","Round 384 to the nearest 10, and then to the nearest 100.","A number rounds to 70 when you round it to the nearest 10. What is the smallest whole number it could be? What is the largest?"],key:["650 to the nearest 10, because 647 sits closer to 650 than to 640. 600 to the nearest 100, because 647 is less than 650, the halfway point between 600 and 700. Partial: one of the two roundings right is approaching.","380 to the nearest 10, because the ones digit 4 is less than 5. 400 to the nearest 100, because 84 is more than 50. Partial: one of the two roundings right is approaching.","65 is the smallest and 74 is the largest. 75 would round up to 80. Partial: one of the smallest and the largest given is approaching."]}]},{id:17,title:"Six Equal Parts",short:"Equal Parts",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.1","3.G.A.2"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the bed. Answer out loud.",desc:"naming the unit fraction for one equal part of a shape, and reading a fraction as a number of parts of that size",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">This garden bed is cut into parts that all have the same area. The shaded parts are planted.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row(F.gridRect(1,6,4,{cell:72})),qs:["The bed is cut into 6 parts of equal area. What fraction of the whole bed is one part? How do you know?","What fraction of the bed is planted? Say what that fraction means using the words parts of size.","Suppose the same bed were cut into 8 parts of equal area instead of 6. Would one part be bigger or smaller than one sixth? Why?"],key:["One sixth, 1/6 (3.G.A.2). The whole bed is split into 6 parts that all have the same area, so one of those parts is 1 of 6, which is 1/6 of the bed. Partial: the fraction right with no reason from the equal areas is approaching.","Four sixths, 4/6 (3.NF.A.1). It means 4 parts, each one of size 1/6. Listen for parts of size 1/6, not just the name of the fraction. Saying it is also 2/3 is correct but is a grade 4 move and is not what is being checked here. Partial: naming the fraction without the words parts of size is approaching.","Smaller (3.NF.A.1). The same bed has to go into more parts, so each part gets a smaller share of it. 1/8 is less than 1/6. Partial: saying smaller with no reason is approaching."]}]},{id:18,title:"Area of the Bed",short:"Area",parts:[{std:["3.MD.C.7"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Every square is one square foot. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding area by multiplying side lengths and by adding the areas of two parts",fig:()=>F.row(areaFig()),qs:["How many square feet is Part A? How did you work it out?","How many square feet is the whole bed, Part A and Part B together? Write it two different ways.","Why does 4 x 7 + 4 x 3 come out the same as 4 x 10?"],key:["28 square feet. Part A is 4 rows of 7 squares, so 4 x 7 = 28. Counting all the squares one at a time gets there too, but note it: the standard is about relating the area to the multiplication. Partial: counting the squares one at a time counts here, and the multiplication is the thing to note.","40 square feet. As 4 x 7 + 4 x 3 = 28 + 12 = 40, and as 4 x 10 = 40. Part B is 4 rows of 3, which is 12. Partial: the right area with only one way written is approaching.","Because 7 + 3 = 10, and the two parts side by side are the whole bed. Splitting a rectangle into two pieces and adding their areas gives the same area as the whole rectangle. Partial: naming 7 + 3 = 10 with nothing said about the two parts making the whole is approaching."]}]},{id:19,title:"The Rain Barrel Report",short:"Main Idea",parts:[{std:["SL.3.2"],n:3,dur:55,read:BARREL,dir:"Listen to the report. Then look at the chart and answer out loud.",desc:"finding the main idea and supporting details of a text read aloud, and reading a chart that goes with it",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Your teacher will read you a short report. Listen for what the whole report is about. The chart comes after.</p>`,fig:()=>F.table(["Roof","Gallons caught from 1 inch of rain"],[["a garden shed","30"],["a garage","160"],["a house","620"]],{caption:"How much a roof catches"}),qs:["What is the main idea of the report?","Give two details from the report that support that main idea.","The chart was not in the report. What does it add, and which number shows it best?"],key:["A rain barrel catches the water running off a roof and saves it for the garden. Count it correct when the answer covers the whole report. A single detail, such as it has a screen on top, is not the main idea","Any two of: it stands under the downspout so roof water pours in instead of running away; half an inch of rain can fill a whole barrel; a screen keeps leaves and insects out; a tap lets you fill a watering can; the water costs nothing; it is there on the dry weeks","It backs up the idea that a roof catches far more water than people expect, with real amounts. The house roof catching 620 gallons from one inch of rain shows it best. Naming the garage at 160 counts too if the student explains it"]}]},{id:20,title:"Explain How to Plant a Row",short:"Explain",parts:[{std:["W.3.2"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative piece explaining how to plant and care for a row of seeds",html:()=>`<p class="big">A second grade class is going to plant its first row of seeds and has asked you how. Write 4 or 5 sentences that explain it to them.</p>${checklist(["Start with a sentence that says what you are explaining","Give the steps and two facts a new planter needs to know","Use linking words: first, next, after that, because, also","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic in an opening sentence, such as: Planting a row of seeds takes four steps (W.3.2)","Develops the topic with facts and details, such as how deep the seeds go, how far apart, watering, and keeping the soil from drying out (W.3.2)","Uses linking words and phrases to connect ideas: first, next, after that, because, also, so (W.3.2)","Provides a concluding statement rather than stopping mid-step (W.3.2)"]}]},{id:21,title:"A Robin on the Lawn",short:"Senses",parts:[{title:"Sense, Brain, Act",std:["3-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. For the last one, draw your model on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"using a model to show how an animal takes in information, processes it, and responds",html:()=>`<p class="big">A robin hops three steps across the wet lawn and stops. She tilts her head so one ear is close to the grass. She holds still for a second. Then she stabs her beak into the ground. She pulls up a worm.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("bluebird",96)}${F.icon("ear",80)}${F.icon("sprout",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which body part took in the information about the worm. What was it sensing?","She did not stab at the first patch of grass she came to. What had to happen inside her first?","Put this on paper as a model. Draw three steps with arrows between them. Put a label on every step."],key:["Her ear, held close to the grass, sensing the faint sound of the worm moving. Accept hearing. (3-LS1-1)","Her brain took the sound information, worked out exactly where under the grass it came from, and decided to strike. (3-LS1-1)","Three parts in order with arrows: ear or hears the worm, then brain, then stabs at the spot. The order is what is rated. A model with nothing in the middle is incomplete. (3-LS1-1)"]},{title:"The Safety of a Flock",std:["3-LS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"building an argument from data that forming a group helps animals survive",html:()=>`<p class="big">Starlings feed on the spring field, sometimes alone and sometimes in big flocks. A class watched both and recorded what happened when a hawk came over.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Starlings</th><th>Seconds before the hawk was spotted</th><th>Hawk attacks that succeeded, out of 10</th></tr>
     <tr><td>one starling feeding alone</td><td>9</td><td>5</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a flock of about two hundred</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which starlings spotted the hawk sooner?","Make an argument that feeding in a flock helps a starling survive. Use a number from the table.","When the flock lifts off it twists and turns together as one huge shape. How could that make it harder for a hawk?"],key:["the flock, which spotted it in 2 seconds against 9 (3-LS2-1)","The claim yes, with the numbers attached: with two hundred birds watching, the hawk was spotted in 2 seconds instead of 9, and only 1 attack in 10 succeeded against 5. A yes with no number is not yet an argument. (3-LS2-1)","A hawk has to pick out one bird to chase, and in a twisting crowd of hundreds that is hard to do, so it may give up or miss. Accept any sensible version of that idea. (3-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Bulbs and Their Young",short:"Traits",parts:[{title:"Passed Down and Shaped",std:["3-LS3-1","3-LS3-2"],n:4,dur:120,dir:"Look at both tables. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading trait data for parents and offspring, naming the variation, and telling when the surroundings shaped a trait",html:()=>`<p class="big">Table 1: a yellow tulip and the four small bulbs that grew off it, planted out the next spring.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plant</th><th>Flower color</th><th>Stem height in cm</th></tr>
     <tr><td>parent bulb</td><td>yellow</td><td>34</td></tr>
     <tr><td>offset A</td><td>yellow</td><td>29</td></tr><tr><td>offset B</td><td>yellow</td><td>36</td></tr>
     <tr><td>offset C</td><td>yellow</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>offset D</td><td>yellow</td><td>38</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Table 2: two more offsets from that same parent bulb, planted in different beds.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Offset</th><th>Bed it was planted in</th><th>Days until it bloomed</th><th>Stem height</th></tr>
     <tr><td>offset E</td><td>sunny bed against the south wall</td><td>62</td><td>35 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>offset F</td><td>cold shady bed under the hedge</td><td>89</td><td>19 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Every offset has a yellow flower, the same as the parent bulb. Where did that trait come from?","The stem heights are all a little different. What is that difference called. Does it mean one came off a different bulb?","In Table 2 both offsets came off the same parent. What made one bloom in 62 days and the other in 89?","So is bloom time decided only by the bulb? Explain."],key:["It was inherited from the parent bulb. A trait the parent has and every offspring has is passed down. (3-LS3-1)","Variation. They all still came off that bulb: inherited traits vary from one offspring to the next, so a group of similar organisms is not identical. (3-LS3-1)","The warmth and the light of the bed it was in. Same parent bulb, different surroundings, different bloom time and height. (3-LS3-2)","No. The environment shaped it too. Listen for the idea that a trait can be influenced by where a living thing grows, not by inheritance alone. (3-LS3-2)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Stone and Survival",short:"Fossils",parts:[{title:"Clues in the Hillside",std:["3-LS4-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table of rock layers. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading fossil data to say which organisms lived in a place and what the place was like",html:()=>`<p class="big">A spring slide took a slice out of the hillside above the garden and showed three layers. A class listed the fossils. The top layer is the youngest.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Fossils found</th></tr>
     <tr><td>top</td><td>grass seeds, a mouse jaw</td></tr>
     <tr><td>middle</td><td>palm leaf prints, a turtle shell piece</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottom</td><td>clam shells, ripple marks turned to stone</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("palm",80)}${F.icon("turtle",80)}${F.icon("shell",80)}</div>`,qs:["Name two organisms that once lived where this hillside now stands.","Was the middle layer a warm wet place or a cold dry one? Use the fossils as your evidence.","Put the three layers in order from oldest to youngest and say how the place changed."],key:["Any two the table names: a grass, a mouse, a palm, a turtle, a clam. (3-LS4-1)","Warm and wet. Palms and turtles live in warm damp places, and naming them is the evidence. An answer with no fossil named is not yet using the data. (3-LS4-1)","bottom, middle, top: from shallow water with clams and ripples, to a warm wet place with palms, to dry grassland. Order and the change both have to be there. (3-LS4-1)"]},{title:"A Useful Difference",std:["3-LS4-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how a variation among individuals of one species gives an advantage",html:()=>`<p class="big">Frogs in the garden pond jump away from a passing heron. A class measured back legs and timed the jumps.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Frogs</th><th>Back leg length</th><th>Jump distance</th><th>Escaped the heron, out of 15 tries</th></tr>
     <tr><td>shorter back legs</td><td>5 cm</td><td>40 cm</td><td>5</td></tr>
     <tr><td>longer back legs</td><td>8 cm</td><td>95 cm</td><td>13</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which frogs jumped further?","Explain why a longer back leg is an advantage for a frog at this pond. Use the table.","The longer-legged frogs also left more eggs behind. How can a trait that helps a frog escape end up giving it more young?"],key:["the longer-legged frogs, 95 cm against 40 cm (3-LS4-2)","A longer leg sends the frog further in one jump, so it can reach water or cover before the heron strikes, and 13 of 15 escaped against 5. The explanation has to lean on the data. (3-LS4-2)","A frog that escapes survives to the next breeding season, so it has more chances to find a mate and lay eggs. Accept any version that links surviving to finding mates or reproducing. (3-LS4-2)"]}]},{id:24,title:"The Late Frost",short:"Habitat",parts:[{title:"Who Survives, and What Would Help",std:["3-LS4-3","3-LS4-4"],n:4,dur:120,dir:"Read the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from data who can survive in a changed habitat, then judging the merit of a solution",html:()=>`<p class="big">After a warm March, a hard frost came in April and sat on the garden for three nights. A class recorded the beds afterwards.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Living thing</th><th>What happened after the frost</th></tr>
     <tr><td>kale, a cold-hardy leaf crop</td><td>unharmed, and growing faster once the weather warmed</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bean seedlings</td><td>leaf edges blackened, and growth stalled for two weeks</td></tr>
     <tr><td>tomato seedlings</td><td>every one dead</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which living thing survived well through the frost? Give the evidence.","Which one could not survive at all. Which one survived less well?","The class wants to cover the beds with light cloth on frosty nights. Would that help the tomatoes? Name one drawback.","A second plan is to wait and plant the tomatoes three weeks later, after frost season. Which plan has more merit, and why?"],key:["The kale. It came through unharmed and then grew faster, so the cold did it no damage. (3-LS4-3)","The tomato seedlings could not survive at all, every one died. The bean seedlings survived less well, blackened edges and two weeks of stalled growth. (3-LS4-3)","Yes, cloth holds some warmth near the plants and keeps frost off the leaves. Then any real drawback: somebody has to put it on and take it off every night, it does not help in a very hard frost, cloth left on in the day blocks light, it can be blown off. A claim with no drawback is half of this standard. (3-LS4-4)","Either plan can earn credit. What is rated is the reasoning: planting later avoids the frost entirely with no nightly work, but it costs three weeks of growing season, while the cloth lets you plant early and takes effort every night. (3-LS4-4)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Weather and Climate",short:"Weather",parts:[{title:"A Season of Weather",std:["3-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud. For the last one, draw your graph on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading and making a graphical display of typical weather for one season",html:()=>`<p class="big">The garden club keeps a record of the lowest overnight temperature each week through spring.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Week</th><th>Lowest night temperature</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>1 C below zero</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2 C</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1 C below zero</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>5 C</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>8 C</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>11 C</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("thermometer",88)}${F.icon("purpleFlower",88)}</div>`,qs:["In which weeks did the temperature drop below freezing?","After which week would you call it safe to plant something frost-tender? Use the table to back up your answer.","Draw a graph of the six weeks, with a line marked at zero. Label both sides."],key:["weeks 1 and 3, both at 1 C below zero (3-ESS2-1)","After week 3, because weeks 4, 5 and 6 all stay above freezing and the trend is upward. Accept week 4 onward with the data used as the reason. (3-ESS2-1)","Six bars or points in week order matching 1 below zero, 2, 1 below zero, 5, 8 and 11, a marked zero line, a label on the week side and a label on the temperature side. A graph with no labels is incomplete. (3-ESS2-1)"]},{title:"Two Parts of the World",std:["3-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information to describe the climates of two different regions of the world",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two regions of the world and how their rain falls through the year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Rain in a year</th><th>When the rain falls</th><th>Summer average</th></tr>
     <tr><td>a region with four even seasons</td><td>95 cm</td><td>spread through every month</td><td>24 C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a monsoon region</td><td>210 cm</td><td>180 cm of it in four months</td><td>31 C</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("rain",80)}${F.icon("globe",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which region gets more rain in a whole year?","In the monsoon region, 180 cm of the 210 cm falls in just four months. What does that mean for the other eight months?","Say one reason why climates differ from one region of the world to another."],key:["the monsoon region, 210 cm against 95 cm (3-ESS2-2)","Those eight months are dry, with only about 30 cm between them, so the region has a long dry stretch and then a short very wet one. (3-ESS2-2)","Any sound reason: how near a region is to the middle of the globe, which way the seasonal winds blow, how near it is to an ocean, how high it sits above sea level, whether mountains block the rain. One solid reason is enough here. (3-ESS2-2)"]},{title:"Ready for the Spring Flood",std:["3-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"making a claim about the merit of a design that reduces the impact of a weather hazard",html:()=>`<p class="big">Spring rain runs off the slope above the garden. It washes the soil out of the top beds. The club digs a shallow curved ditch across the slope. It sits just above the garden. A low bank of soil runs along its downhill side.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("rain",88)}${F.icon("wateringCan",88)}</div>`,qs:["Which weather hazard is the ditch and bank meant to reduce?","Make a claim. Does the ditch and bank have merit? Give your reason.","Name one thing this design does not protect the garden from."],key:["runoff from heavy spring rain washing soil out of the beds (3-ESS3-1)","Yes, with a reason attached: the ditch catches the water running down the slope and the bank holds it, so it soaks in or runs off slowly instead of pouring through the beds. A yes with no reason is not yet a claim. (3-ESS3-1)","Any real limit: rain falling straight onto the garden itself, a storm bigger than the ditch can hold, the ditch filling with silt and needing cleaning, frost, hail, wind, water coming from a different direction. (3-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Carts and Hoops",short:"Forces",parts:[{title:"Balanced or Not",std:["3-PS2-1"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"planning an investigation of balanced and unbalanced forces acting on an object",html:()=>`<p class="big">A garden cart full of compost stands on the sloped path. Its wheels are chocked with two bricks, and it does not move.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("box",96)}${F.icon("sprout",80)}</div>`,qs:["The slope pulls the cart downhill and it stays still. Are the forces on it balanced or unbalanced? How do you know?","One brick is kicked out and the cart rolls. What changed about the forces?","Plan an investigation to find out whether a fuller cart needs a bigger chock to hold it. What will you change, and what will you measure?","Which conditions do you hold fixed for every run?"],key:["Balanced. The bricks push back exactly as hard as the slope pulls, and the cart standing still is the evidence. (3-PS2-1)","One brick no longer balances the pull of the slope, so the forces became unbalanced and the cart rolled downhill. (3-PS2-1)","A plan with one thing changed and one thing measured: load the cart with one more bucket of compost at a time (the change) and find the smallest chock that still holds it, or count the bricks needed (the measure). (3-PS2-1)","Any controlled variable that fits the plan: the same cart, the same stretch of path and slope, the same chock material, the same wheels, the same dry weather. (3-PS2-1)"]},{title:"Predict the Next Second",std:["3-PS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a pattern in motion data to predict where an object will be later",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class rolls a hoop along the flat garden path and marks where its front edge is at the end of each second.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Seconds</th><th>Distance from the start</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>4 m</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>8 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>12 m</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>16 m</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern you can see in these numbers.","The path is 32 m long. When will the hoop reach the end, and how do you know?","What could happen on the path that would make your prediction wrong?"],key:["It travels 4 m in every second, the same distance each time. The hoop is rolling at a steady speed. (3-PS2-2)","at 8 seconds, because 32 m divided into 4 m steps is eight seconds. The reason has to be the pattern, not a guess. (3-PS2-2)","A new force acting on the hoop: the path tilts or turns gravelly, a puddle slows it, it hits a stone, wind pushes it, someone gives it another push. A pattern only predicts while nothing changes. (3-PS2-2)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Pulling Without Touching",short:"Magnets",parts:[{title:"Ask About the Pull",std:["3-PS2-3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. Your two questions have to be questions, not statements.",desc:"asking questions about cause and effect in magnetic interactions between objects not touching",html:()=>`<p class="big">A steel plant label is pushed into the soil of a raised bed. A strong magnet is run along underneath the wooden side board. The label leans and then slides toward it through the soil.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("key",88)}${F.icon("pencil",80)}</div>`,qs:["The magnet never touches the label, yet the label moves. What does that tell you about this force?","Ask two questions about this that a test could answer.","An aluminum label in the same spot does not move at all. What does that tell you about which materials this force acts on?"],key:["The force acts across a distance and straight through the wood and the soil, with no contact between the objects. (3-PS2-3)","Any two real investigable questions: how thick can the board be before it stops working, does wet soil change it, does a bigger label move further, how close does the magnet have to be, does a second magnet make it move more. A statement is not a question, so ask once more. (3-PS2-3)","A magnet pulls steel and iron but not aluminum, so the force depends on what the object is made of, not just on the magnet. (3-PS2-3)"]},{title:"A Problem for a Magnet",std:["3-PS2-4","3-5-ETS1-1"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud. For the third one you may write your two sentences on your paper.",desc:"defining a design problem that magnets can solve, with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">The seed beds were made from old boards. Steel staples came out of them into the loose soil. A staple in a bed can cut a hand that is planting. Sifting the whole bed by hand takes a whole afternoon.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("key",80)}${F.icon("box",80)}${F.icon("sprout",72)}</div>`,qs:["Finish this sentence out loud, in one go: The problem is.","Why is a magnet a sensible idea here? Would the same tool find a dropped plastic plant tag in the same soil?","Write two tests your tool must pass for you to call it a success.","You may use three things only. A rake handle, one magnet, and two zip ties. You have one afternoon to build it and use it. What limits does that put on your design?"],key:["A problem statement about getting steel staples out of the bed soil so nobody is cut while planting. The problem has to be about the staples in the soil, not about the old boards. (3-PS2-4)","The staples are steel, and a magnet pulls steel without touching it, so it can draw them up out of loose soil without sifting by hand. A plastic tag is not pulled by a magnet, so no. (3-PS2-4)","Two criteria, written as what the tool must do: pull up every staple, reach the depth people plant at, work faster than sifting by hand, hold the staples while you lift, not damage the seedlings already in. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Names the limits as limits: only three materials, only one magnet, only one afternoon. Materials, time and cost are the constraints to listen for. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Compare and Test",short:"Design",parts:[{title:"Three Ways to Do It",std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table and the three rules. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing several possible solutions against stated criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Small seeds have to be sown evenly along a row. Three tools are tried, each on a four meter row.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Seeds per meter, high and low</th><th>Minutes for the row</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
     <tr><td>sowing by hand</td><td>from 4 to 19</td><td>3</td><td>nothing</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a shaker jar with holes</td><td>from 9 to 13</td><td>2</td><td>1 dollar</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a push seeder on wheels</td><td>from 10 to 11</td><td>1</td><td>45 dollars</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The rules for success: between 8 and 14 seeds a meter, under 3 minutes a row, cost under 10 dollars.</p>`,qs:["Which tool spaces the seeds most evenly?","Which tool meets all three rules? Explain using the table.","Pick a tool you would not use. Then name the rule it fails."],key:["the push seeder, 10 to 11 seeds a meter (3-5-ETS1-2)","The shaker jar. It gives 9 to 13 seeds a meter, takes 2 minutes, and costs 1 dollar. Another choice counts only if the student argues it against all three rules using the table. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any reasoned rejection with the rule named: sowing by hand fails the evenness rule, since 4 and 19 are outside 8 to 14, and the push seeder fails the cost rule at 45 dollars. Naming the rule is the part being rated. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{title:"Make the Test Fair",std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"judging whether a test is fair, naming controlled variables, and identifying a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two soil mixes were tested to see which grows stronger seedlings.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Mix</th><th>Seeds planted</th><th>Where the tray sat</th><th>Watering</th></tr>
     <tr><td>mix A, sandy</td><td>24 bean seeds</td><td>sunny bench</td><td>every day</td></tr>
     <tr><td>mix B, with compost</td><td>24 bean seeds</td><td>sunny bench</td><td>every third day</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Is this a fair comparison? Say what is right and what is wrong about it.","Name two things that are already kept the same here. One more you would add.","In mix A the water ran straight through and out of the bottom every single time. Name that weak spot. Then say what you would change about the mix."],key:["No. The soil and the watering were both different, so stronger seedlings cannot be pinned on the mix. Note that the seeds, the number and the spot were controlled, which is a good start. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Already the same: 24 seeds each, the same kind of seed, the same sunny bench. One more to add: the same watering schedule, the same amount of water, the same tray size, the same planting depth. (3-5-ETS1-3)","The failure point is that mix A does not hold water, so it drains before the roots can use it. Any improvement aimed there: add compost or peat to hold moisture, water less at a time but more often, add a tray underneath to catch and hold water. An improvement somewhere else does not count. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Maps and Places",short:"Maps",parts:[{std:["HSS-3.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Use the map grid and the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a map grid and a table to describe physical and human features and where they sit",html:()=>`<p class="big">A map grid of the town around the community garden. North is the top row.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>west</th><th>middle</th><th>east</th></tr>
     <tr><th>north</th><td>${F.icon("mountain",54)}<br>hill</td><td>${F.icon("sprout",54)}<br>community garden</td><td>${F.icon("books",54)}<br>library</td></tr>
     <tr><th>middle</th><td>${F.icon("tree",54)}<br>city woods</td><td>${F.icon("cabin",54)}<br>school</td><td>${F.icon("bread",54)}<br>shops</td></tr>
     <tr><th>south</th><td>${F.icon("drop",54)}<br>river</td><td>${F.icon("bridge",54)}<br>river bridge</td><td>${F.icon("bus",54)}<br>train station</td></tr></table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Place</th><th>Ground it covers</th></tr>
     <tr><td>community garden</td><td>1 acre</td></tr><tr><td>city woods</td><td>12 acres</td></tr>
     <tr><td>school grounds</td><td>4 acres</td></tr><tr><td>train station</td><td>2 acres</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What lies directly north of the school?","Which two directions do you walk to get from the school to the train station?","Which places here are part of the land itself? Which did people build? Name one of each. Then look at this map. Name one thing that shows people changing the land. They changed it in order to use a resource.","Which place in the table covers the most ground? About how many times bigger is it than the garden?"],key:["the community garden (HSS-3.1)","south and east, in either order. The train station sits in the south row and the east column. Partial: one of the two directions is approaching. (HSS-3.1)","Physical: the hill, the city woods, the river. Human-made: the garden, the library, the school, the shops, the bridge, the train station. For the last part, any of: the bridge built across the river, the garden beds dug into the ground, the rail line and station laid across the town, the woods kept as city woods rather than built on. One of each of the three is what is being checked. Partial: two of the three parts given is approaching. (HSS-3.1)","the city woods, 12 acres, which is about twelve times the size of the one-acre garden. Partial: the woods named with no comparison to the garden is approaching. (HSS-3.1)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Long Ago in This Place",short:"Long Ago",parts:[{title:"The First Nations Here",std:["HSS-3.2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. Use what is on the screen and what you have learned.",desc:"describing how the American Indian nations of California worked with the seasons and used folklore to pass knowledge on, and how a nation is part of the state today",html:()=>`<p class="big">California nations watched the year closely. They knew which month the greens came up. They knew when the fish ran. They knew when the seeds and nuts were ready. They moved and worked with that calendar rather than against it. Each nation also had its own folklore. These were stories told out loud at set times of the year. Some of those stories taught about the seasons. They taught the very things a child needed to know.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",88)}${F.icon("fish",80)}</div>`,qs:["The screen names three things nations watched for through the year. Name all three, and say what they did with that calendar.","A nation had folklore stories. They taught children about the seasons. A child had to remember something for life. Telling a story is a good way to teach. Why does it work?","Does all of this belong to the past? Give one way a nation is part of California today."],key:["Which month the greens came up. When the fish ran. When the seeds and nuts were ready. They moved and worked with that calendar rather than against it. Partial: two of the three signs named, or all three with nothing said about moving and working with the calendar, is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","A story is easy to remember and easy to pass on, so the knowledge travelled with it, and a child who could tell the story carried the knowledge too. Accept any sensible answer about stories being easier to remember or easier to hand down than a list of facts. Partial: saying stories are fun or interesting, with nothing about remembering or handing on, is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","No. Any true present-day answer: nations have their own tribal governments, they work on land and water care, there are language classes and cultural centers, people hold cultural events, members live and work across the state. Partial: saying no with no present-day example is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","Not scored, ask it anyway: which American Indian nation or nations have their homeland where this student lives? The answer is different for every family, so there is no key for it and it stays out of the rating. Tell the student the name if they do not know it, write it on the record sheet, and make it something to teach before the next check."]},{title:"A Town Over Time",std:["HSS-3.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the timeline. Answer out loud.",desc:"sequencing local historical events and describing the mark each period left on the land",html:()=>`<p class="big">A timeline for the town of Cedar Fork.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1806</td><td>an exploring party maps the two rivers and moves on</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1847</td><td>a camp grows up where two rivers meet</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1869</td><td>a levee is built along the river to keep spring floods off the town</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1903</td><td>a cannery opens, and rail spurs run out to the orchards</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1952</td><td>a dam upstream ends the spring floods</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2008</td><td>part of the levee is opened and the ground behind it becomes a wetland park</td></tr></table>
      <p class="big">Some people only passed through Cedar Fork and some stayed. The 1806 party mapped the rivers and went on. The families of 1847 stayed, built the first houses, and brought their own language, their own songs and their own church with them. Later the cannery drew workers from Mexico, Italy and Japan, and a Buddhist temple and an Italian social hall went up on the same street as that first church.</p>`,qs:["Put these three in order, earliest first: the dam, the levee, the cannery.","Which event changed the river’s edge. What did it do to it?","Some people only visited this place and some settled in it. Name one of each from the timeline. Then name one tradition or faith that the people who settled brought with them."],key:["levee, cannery, dam. Partial: two of the three in the right order is approaching. (HSS-3.3)","the levee in 1869, which walled off the river’s edge so it could not spread over the town. Accept the 2008 opening as a second correct answer, since that changed the edge again. Partial: naming the levee with no account of what it did to the edge is approaching. (HSS-3.3)","Visited and went on: the 1806 exploring party that mapped the rivers. Settled: the families of 1847 who built the first houses, or the cannery workers from Mexico, Italy and Japan. Traditions and faiths on the page: their own language, their own songs, their church, the Buddhist temple, the Italian social hall. All three parts are needed for a full answer. Partial: two of the three parts given is approaching. (HSS-3.3)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Rules and Work",short:"Rules",parts:[{title:"Rules, Laws and Government",std:["HSS-3.4"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"telling rules from laws and naming the basic parts and levels of United States government",html:()=>`<p class="big">The community garden has a rule. Water your own plot only on your own day. The state has a law about a dry year. It limits how much water a town may draw from the river.</p>
      <p class="big">A government does not only make laws. Somebody breaks one. A court hears the case. It decides what follows. It may be a fine to pay. It may be work to put right what was damaged. It may be a licence taken away. For the most serious crimes it may be time in prison.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wateringCan",88)}${F.icon("flag",88)}</div>`,qs:["What is the difference between the garden’s rule and the state water law?","Why do both the garden and the state need something written down about water?","The United States government has three parts. Which one writes laws, and which one puts them to work?","Somebody breaks a law. Who decides what happens to them? Name two things that can follow."],key:["The garden rule is set by the gardeners and applies in the garden. The state law is made by a government, applies to every town drawing from that river, and the government decides what happens when it is broken. Partial: describing the garden rule with nothing said about who makes the state law is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","Because water is limited and many people want it, so there has to be an agreed way to share it rather than whoever takes most getting most. Accept any answer about fairness or a limited resource. Partial: saying water matters with no account of sharing a limited supply is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","the legislative branch, the state legislature or Congress, makes the laws, and the executive branch carries them out. Partial: one of the two parts named is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","A court decides. Any two from the page: a fine to pay, work to put right what was damaged, a licence taken away, time in prison for the most serious crimes. Partial: the court named with only one consequence given is approaching. (HSS-3.4)"]},{title:"Choices and the Local Economy",std:["HSS-3.5"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"basic economic reasoning about cost, price, scarcity, and goods and services",html:()=>`<p class="big">The garden club grows seedlings and sells them at a spring sale to pay for next year’s seed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr>
     <tr><td>what the seed, soil and pots cost the club</td><td>36 dollars</td></tr>
     <tr><td>seedlings they have to sell</td><td>60</td></tr>
     <tr><td>price for one seedling</td><td>2 dollars</td></tr></table>`,qs:["If every seedling sells, how much comes in? how much is left after paying the 36 dollars?","The club only grew 20 seedlings one year and 70 people wanted one. Describe what happens and name what we call it when there is not enough to go around.","The club sells seedlings and it also plants them for people who cannot dig. Which is a good and which is a service?","Give one job from your own town and sort it: good or service?"],key:["120 dollars comes in, since 60 times 2 is 120, and 84 dollars is left after the 36 dollars of costs. Partial: the 120 dollars right with no amount left over is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","The seedlings sell out fast and most people get none, or the club could raise the price. That is scarcity: less of something than people want. Accept scarcity explained in the student’s own words. Partial: describing the sell-out with no name for there not being enough to go around is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","A seedling is a good, something you can hold. Planting it for someone is a service, work someone does for you. Partial: one of the two sorted right is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","Any real local job sorted correctly: a baker makes a good, a nurse provides a service, a farmer produces a good, a mail carrier provides a service. Partial: a real job named with the good or service label wrong is approaching. (HSS-3.5)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[2].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level story read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Put It Together",std:["RI.4.1"],needs:["RI.3.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details and examples from the text.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The text never says that a garden with no pollinators would grow fewer apples. Use details from the text to explain why that could happen.</li><li>Use the text to explain why a beekeeper might be paid to bring hives to an apple orchard in spring.</li></ol>${beeHtml(true)}`,key:["Pollen has to travel from one flower to another for a seed to start, pollen cannot walk, and most of the time an animal carries it. The text also says apples need that help. Put together, a garden with no pollinator would have nothing to move its pollen, so fewer flowers would start fruit. The text never states this, so the student has to combine the details. (RI.4.1, inference backed by details and examples)","Apples are named in the text as a plant that needs pollinator help, and a bee moves pollen as it goes flower to flower drinking nectar. More bees in the orchard means more flowers get pollen, so the grower pays to have the hives there in spring when the trees flower. (RI.4.1, inference with details)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Unlike Pieces",std:["4.NF.A.2"],needs:["3.NF.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud and tell how you know. You can write on your paper.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which is greater, 3/4 of a watering can or 5/8 of the same can?</li><li>Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 2/5 ____ 1/2</li><li>Is 7/12 of a can more or less than half a can? Use half as your benchmark.</li></ol>`,key:["3/4. In eighths, 3/4 is 6/8 and 6/8 is more than 5/8. (4.NF.A.2, comparing fractions with different numerators and denominators)","2/5 &lt; 1/2. In tenths, 2/5 is 4/10 and 1/2 is 5/10, so 2/5 is less. (4.NF.A.2)","More than half. Half of 12 is 6, and 7 is more than 6, so 7/12 is just over half. Accept the comparison in twelfths: 1/2 is 6/12 and 7/12 is more. (4.NF.A.2, comparing to a benchmark)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Read Between the Lines",std:["RL.4.1"],needs:["RL.3.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The story never says that Dev was worried about his row. How do you know that he was? Give two details from the story.</li><li>Why do you think Dev brought Ms. Reyes out to look at lunch? Use a detail from the story.</li></ol>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["He was worried, backed by two details: every other row had green in it while his was bare dirt; he watered it anyway every morning before the bell; and he did not tell anyone that he had started counting the days. (RL.4.1, inference with details)","He wanted someone else to see the sprouts and tell him whether nine was enough. The detail is that he said “Nine out of twenty” and “he was not sure if that was good,” and Ms. Reyes answered him. (RL.4.1, inference with a detail)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Eighths on the Plot",std:["4.MD.B.4"],needs:["3.MD.B.4"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">The class measured five bean seedlings to the nearest eighth of an inch. The heights were 1 1/8, 1 1/2, 1 1/2, 1 7/8, and 2 inches.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>Make a line plot of those five heights. Mark your scale in eighths of an inch from 1 to 2.</li><li>How much taller is the tallest seedling than the shortest one?</li><li>Two of the seedlings measured the same. How much taller is the tallest seedling than one of those two?</li></ol>`,key:["The scale needs every eighth from 1 to 2 marked and labeled: 1, 1 1/8, 1 1/4, 1 3/8, 1 1/2, 1 5/8, 1 3/4, 1 7/8, 2. Accept 1 2/8, 1 4/8, and 1 6/8 for the quarters and the half. The marks go 1 above 1 1/8, 2 above 1 1/2, 1 above 1 7/8, and 1 above 2. Five marks in all, and the ticks with no measurement still have to be on the scale. (4.MD.B.4, a line plot in eighths)","7/8 of an inch. Write 2 as 1 8/8, then 1 8/8 take away 1 1/8 leaves 7/8. (4.MD.B.4, subtraction of fractions from a line plot)","1/2 inch. The two that matched were 1 1/2, and 2 take away 1 1/2 is 1/2. Accept 4/8. (4.MD.B.4)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Grown-Up Grammar",std:["L.4.1"],needs:["L.3.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which sentence is right? A: The sprouts could of come up sooner. B: The sprouts could have come up sooner.</li><li>Put these words in the order that sounds right in English: <b>three / green / small / beans</b></li><li>Change this sentence so it tells what was happening yesterday afternoon: Dev waters the last row.</li></ol>`,key:["B. “Could of” is not a correct form, so the modal auxiliary needs have. (L.4.1, modal auxiliaries and common usage errors)","three small green beans. Number, then size, then color, then the noun. (L.4.1, ordering adjectives within a sentence)","Dev was watering the last row. (L.4.1, past progressive verb tense)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.3.1","RL.3.4","RI.3.1","RI.3.9","W.3.8","RL.4.1","RI.4.1"];const FRAC_DENS=[1,2,3,4,6,8];let fracOn={};function fracHtml(){return FRAC_DENS.map(d=>{const on=fracOn[d]||0;let r=`<div class="fs-row">`;for(let k=0;k<d;k++)r+=`<button class="fs-part${k<on?" on":""}" data-d="${d}" data-k="${k}">${d===1?"1 whole":"1/"+d}</button>`;return r+"</div>"}).join("")+`<p class="small">Click a piece to shade up to it. Click the last shaded piece to clear the row.</p>`}function lineHtml(){const rows=[[2,"halves"],[3,"thirds"],[4,"fourths"],[6,"sixths"],[8,"eighths"]];return`<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px">`+rows.map(r=>`<div style="text-align:center"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0">0 to 1 in ${r[1]}</p>${F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:1/r[0],hideNumbers:true,ticks:[[0,"0"],[1,"1"]],width:520})}</div>`).join("")+`</div><p class="small">Count the equal spaces, not the tick marks. The 0 tick is where you start counting from.</p>`}function plotHtml(){return F.table(["Step","What to do"],[["1","Draw a line and mark the whole inches on it."],["2","Mark every quarter inch in between, even the ones with no measurement."],["3","Label every mark, and keep the spaces between marks the same size."],["4","Put one X above a mark for each measurement at that length."],["5","Stack the X marks straight up so they are easy to count."]])+`<div class="figure" style="margin-top:8px">${xPlot({min:1,max:2,step:.25,counts:{1.25:2,1.5:1,2:3},labels:{1:"1",1.25:"1 1/4",1.5:"1 1/2",1.75:"1 3/4",2:"2"},width:460,title:"An example plot, in inches",alt:"An example line plot in quarter inches from 1 to 2, with two X marks above 1 and a quarter, one above 1 and a half, none above 1 and three quarters, and three above 2."})}</div>
   <p class="small">This example is not the sprout data. It only shows how a line plot is put together.</p>`}function chunkHtml(){return F.table(["Word part","What it does"],[["re-","again or back: replant"],["un-","not or the opposite of: unripe"],["mis-","wrongly: mislabel"],["-ness","the state of being: dampness"],["-ful","full of: careful"],["-less","without: rootless"],["-tion","the act or result of: direction"],["Compound word","two whole words joined: sunflower"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the prefix and the suffix with your finger, read the middle part, then put the word back together.</p>`}function figsHtml(){return`<ul class="starters"><li>Literal means the words mean exactly what they say. “She went out in her boots” is literal.</li><li>Nonliteral means the words paint a picture instead. “Her heart did a hop” is nonliteral.</li><li>Ask: could this really happen? If not, what picture is the writer putting in my head?</li><li>Then say what it really means in your own plain words.</li></ul>`}const SENT_SET={opinion:"opinion",facts:"W.3.2",story:"story","W.3.1":"opinion","W.3.2":"W.3.2","W.3.3":"story","W.3.8":"W.3.8","SL.3.2":"SL.3.2","SL.3.4":"SL.3.4","RI.3.9":"RI.3.9"};function sentSet(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];for(const p of ps){const a=p.alt&&p.alt.key||p.wk||p.wt;if(a&&SENT_SET[a])return SENT_SET[a];const hit=(p.std||[]).find(c=>SENT_SET[c]);if(hit)return SENT_SET[hit]}return"opinion"}function starters(st){const k=sentSet(st);if(k==="story")return["I had almost given up on ____.","First, ____.","Next, ____.","“____,” said ____.","Finally, ____."];if(k==="W.3.8")return["The text says ____.","What pollen does: ____.","Who moves pollen: ____."];if(k==="SL.3.4")return["Our school garden is ____.","One thing you should know is ____.","The chart says ____.","If you stand by the beds you can ____.","That is our garden."];if(k==="RI.3.9")return["Both texts say ____.","Text 1 says ____.","Text 2 says ____.","That detail is only in Text ____."];if(k==="W.3.2")return["To plant a row of seeds you ____.","First, ____.","Next, ____.","You have to ____ because ____.","That is how ____."];if(k==="SL.3.2")return["The report is mostly about ____.","One detail that shows it is ____.","Another detail is ____.","The chart adds ____."];return["The school should pick ____ because ____.","One reason is ____.","For example, ____.","That is why ____."]}function sentHtml(st){return`<ul class="starters">${starters(st).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={chunk:{name:"Word part chart",std:["RF.3.3"],html:chunkHtml},nline:{name:"Fraction number lines",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.NF.A.2","3.NF.A.3","4.NF.A.2"],html:lineHtml},frac:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.NF.A.3","3.G.A.2","4.NF.A.2"],html:fracHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll(".fs-part").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const d=+x.dataset.d,k=+x.dataset.k;fracOn[d]=fracOn[d]===k+1?0:k+1;r()})},plot:{name:"Line plot card",std:["3.MD.B.4","4.MD.B.4"],html:plotHtml},figs:{name:"Literal and nonliteral card",std:["RL.3.4"],html:figsHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence starters",std:["W.3.1","W.3.2","W.3.3","W.3.8","SL.3.2","SL.3.4","RI.3.9"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"chunk",std:["RF.3.3"],msg:"The word part chart names each prefix and suffix and gives one of the words on the list as its example, so count the word reading as supported, not independent."},{t:"nline",std:["3.NF.A.2"],msg:"The number line tool already shows the equal spaces from 0 to 1, so the partitioning is done for the student. Rate on whether the student can name the point as a fraction and say what one space is called."},{t:"nline",std:["3.NF.A.3","4.NF.A.2"],msg:"Lined-up number lines let the student see which fraction is farther from 0, so count the comparisons as supported, not independent."},{t:"frac",std:["3.NF.A.3","4.NF.A.2"],msg:"Fraction strips show the piece sizes and the equal shading, so rate on whether the student can explain what the strips show rather than on the comparison itself."},{t:"plot",std:["3.MD.B.4","4.MD.B.4"],msg:"The line plot card lists the steps and shows a finished example plot, so count the building of the plot as supported. Rate on whether the measurements landed on the right marks."},{t:"figs",std:["RL.3.4"],msg:"The card explains what literal and nonliteral mean, so count the sorting part of each answer as supported and rate the explanation the student gives."},{t:"sent",std:["W.3.1","W.3.3"],msg:"Sentence starters supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.3.8"],msg:"Sentence starters give the student the note categories, so rate on the facts chosen and on how brief the notes are."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.3.4"],msg:"The frames supply the order of the report, so count the organization check as supported and rate the facts, the detail, and the pace."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.3.9"],msg:"The frames tell the student to pull from each text and to name which one. Rate on the content of the answer."},{t:"passage",std:PASSAGE_STD,msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"nline",std:["3.NF.A.1"],msg:"The number line tool already shows the whole cut into equal spaces, so the partitioning is done for the student. Rate on whether the student can name one space as a unit fraction and say what a/b is made of."},{t:"frac",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.G.A.2"],msg:"Fraction strips show the equal parts and their sizes, so the equal-area idea is handed to the student. Rate on the explanation rather than on the naming."},{t:"sent",std:["W.3.2"],msg:"Sentence starters supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.3.2"],msg:"The frames tell the student that there is a main idea, that details support it, and that the chart adds something. Rate on the content of each answer, not on the shape of it."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
